*****************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

1. Complex types

This is the day-to-day win. State lives as member variables, no re-query needed:

apex
public class OpportunityScorer implements Queueable {

    private List<Opportunity> opps;              // sObjects, directly
    private ScoringConfig config;                // custom Apex class
    private Map<Id, List<OpportunityLineItem>> linesByOpp;

    public OpportunityScorer(List<Opportunity> opps, ScoringConfig config) {
        this.opps = opps;
        this.config = config;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
        for (Opportunity o : opps) {
            o.Score__c = config.score(o);
        }
        update opps;
    }
}

With @future you'd pass Set<Id>, re-query everything, and rebuild the config from custom metadata inside the method.

The constraint: everything held must be serializable. No Http, HttpRequest, Savepoint, Database.QueryLocator, or anonymous inner classes capturing non-serializable scope. Mark fields transient if they shouldn't cross the boundary. Total serialized state counts against heap.

