3. Chaining
apex
public class StepOne implements Queueable {
    public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
        // work
        System.enqueueJob(new StepTwo());     // one child, runs after this commits
    }
}

Rules that matter:

One child job per parent execution. A second enqueueJob throws LimitException.
Chain depth is unlimited in production, capped at 5 in Developer/Trial orgs. That asymmetry means an unguarded recursive chain passes sandbox testing and runs forever in prod. Always gate on a terminating condition and a hard depth counter.
Chained jobs are not guaranteed to run immediately — they queue.

public class RecursiveJob implements Queueable {
    private Integer depth;
    public RecursiveJob(Integer depth) { this.depth = depth; }

    public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
        List<Account> batch = [SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Needs_Sync__c = true LIMIT 200];
        if (batch.isEmpty()) return;

        process(batch);

        if (depth < 50) {                       // hard cap, not just "work remaining"
            System.enqueueJob(new RecursiveJob(depth + 1));
        }
    }
}

