Transaction Finalizers

The gap Queueable had until API v53: if execute() threw, the transaction rolled back and you had no hook to log, notify, or retry. Finalizer fills it.

public class SyncFinalizer implements Finalizer {

    private Id recordId;
    private Integer attempt;

    public SyncFinalizer(Id recordId, Integer attempt) {
        this.recordId = recordId;
        this.attempt = attempt;
    }

    public void execute(FinalizerContext ctx) {

        if (ctx.getResult() == ParentJobResult.SUCCESS) {
            update new Account(Id = recordId, Sync_Status__c = 'Synced');
            return;
        }

        // UNHANDLED_EXCEPTION — the parent's DML was rolled back,
        // but this runs in its own transaction, so this DML commits.
        Exception e = ctx.getException();

        insert new Integration_Log__c(
            Record_Id__c  = recordId,
            Error_Type__c = e.getTypeName(),
            Detail__c     = e.getMessage() + '\n' + e.getStackTraceString(),
            Job_Id__c     = ctx.getAsyncApexJobId()
        );

        if (attempt < 3) {
            System.enqueueJob(new SyncJob(recordId, attempt + 1), attempt * 2);
        }
    }
}

Attached at the top of execute():

public class SyncJob implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

    private Id recordId;
    private Integer attempt;

    public SyncJob(Id recordId, Integer attempt) {
        this.recordId = recordId;
        this.attempt = attempt;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
        System.attachFinalizer(new SyncFinalizer(recordId, attempt));

        // If this throws, the finalizer still runs.
        HttpResponse res = new Http().send(buildRequest());
        if (res.getStatusCode() != 200) {
            throw new CalloutException('ERP returned ' + res.getStatusCode());
        }
    }
}

Key properties:

Runs in its own transaction, after the parent commits or rolls back. That's why its DML survives when the parent's didn't.
Guaranteed to run on both success and unhandled exception — including governor limit exceptions, which nothing else catches.
ctx.getResult() returns ParentJobResult.SUCCESS or UNHANDLED_EXCEPTION; getException() gives the throwable; getAsyncApexJobId() and getRequestId() support log correlation.
One finalizer per Queueable. Attaching a second throws.
It can enqueue one job, which is what makes retry-with-backoff possible — and is also how you keep a chain alive past a failure, since a failed parent can't enqueue anything itself.
Attach it first thing in execute(), before anything that might throw.
Only Queueable supports finalizers. Not @future, not Batch.

When each one still wins
Situation	                                                                                                          Choice
Anything new and async	                                                                                          Queueable
Need retry / guaranteed error logging	                                                                            Queueable + Finalizer
Mixed DML split, one-line trigger call	                                                                          @future (marginally simpler) or Queueable
Record volume past ~10k, need per-chunk limit reset	                                                              Batch
Time-based kickoff	                                                                                              Schedulable, usually launching a Batch or Queueable

*********************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************
The honest summary: @future has no remaining advantage except brevity in trivial cases. Everything it does, Queueable does with observability attached. New code should default to Queueable, and the presence of @future in a codebase is usually a dating marker rather than a design decision.
